<body>
<h1>Ingress-derived-only: the gateway is the only place identity is ever decided</h1>

<div class="col root"><div class="card red"><div class="t">Agent's own request arrives</div><div class="d">carrying whatever identity claim it wants</div></div>
<div class="arrow">↓</div>
<div class="card"><div class="t">STEP 1, Strip every client-supplied identity header, unconditionally</div><div class="d">Before any other logic runs. A client-supplied copy of an identity claim is treated as spoofing by definition, it never even reaches a decision.</div>
<div class="d">This is the step most home-grown gateways skip, trusting a header the caller itself set.</div></div>
<div class="arrow">↓</div>
<div class="card"><div class="t">STEP 2, Resolve real identity server-side, from the credential that actually authenticated</div><div class="d">Not from anything the client claims. The credential is looked up against a store that distinguishes a human, a workspace, and one specific agent instance.</div>
<div class="d">Each agent instance gets its own row, independently revocable, independently attributable, never shared.</div></div>
<div class="arrow">↓</div>
<div class="card"><div class="t">STEP 3, Re-stamp the identity headers from the VERIFIED row, only now</div><div class="d">Every downstream handler reads a workspace/agent identity header, but the value now came from the database row the credential resolved to, never from the client.</div>
<div class="d">One ingress point re-stamps, everywhere else just trusts, no downstream code re-derives identity on its own.</div></div>
<div class="arrow">↓</div>
<div class="card green"><div class="t">STEP 4, Deterministic policy decides, outside the model entirely</div><div class="d">A policy-as-code engine (Rego, Cedar) evaluates the verified identity against granted capabilities, allow, escalate for approval, or deny. Fail-closed on any error.</div></div></div>


</body>
